        {
          "kind": "background",
          "text": "The execution engine already has an event bus. `EventBuilder` produces `PipelineEvent` objects and `build_emitter()` fans them out to three backends — JSONL, Logfire and SignalBridge — behind `EventEmitter`, a structural protocol whose whole surface is `emit`/`aclose`. That shape is why a fourth consumer costs nothing at the call sites: nothing dispatching events needs to know it exists."
        },
        {
          "kind": "background",
          "text": "The same directory is a governed boundary. ADR-0007 declared the events JSONL a published contract consumed cross-language by `cobuilder-frontend` and the sidecar, and ADR-0008 made the gate on `cobuilder/execution_engine/events/` path-based, so any change under that path needs a decision record naming its consumers — even one that adds no fields."
        },
        {
          "kind": "background",
          "text": "Above the bus sits the runner. `pipeline_runner.py` walks a hand-authored DOT graph, dispatches agents through `DispatchConfig`, and waits for signal files; `guardian.py` scores the results. Both assume the task tree was decided in advance by a template — there is no path by which a planning agent can grow the tree while the run is in flight."
        },
        {
          "kind": "intuition",
          "text": "Take one worker session that makes forty tool calls. Today it leaves exactly one artifact behind: a signal file holding `{\"status\": \"success\", \"files_changed\": [...]}`. With `activegraph_enabled=True` and `activegraph_store=\"sqlite:///run.db\"` set on `EventBusConfig`, the same session leaves an `action` object plus forty `session_event` objects joined to it by `part_of_action` — every message, thought, call and result, in order, from which `Runtime.load` reconstructs the run exactly. With both fields at their defaults (`False` and `None`) the backend is never constructed and the run is byte-for-byte what it was before."
        },
        {
          "kind": "intuition",
          "text": "Spike 3's loop is the same idea running upward. `submit_goal(\"...\", briefing)` dispatches one planner-role session; the planner explores the repo and calls `plan_create` with milestones and tasks; `AgRunner` converts that payload into `milestone`/`task` objects and dispatches the ready ones as tracked `asyncio.Task`s. The concurrency is real rather than nominal — the PR reports the two resulting task sessions spawning about 20 ms apart against a local model, which serial dispatch could not produce."
        }
      ],
      "voice": "Here is the problem this change is really about. Today the pipeline keeps its memory in four different places. The graph file holds the shape of the work and gets edited in place as nodes run. Checkpoint files hold enough to resume. Signal files hold what each worker said when it finished. And a separate issue tracker holds the human view. None of these holds the whole story, and crucially, when a worker session ends, everything that happened inside it is thrown away. Only the final note survives. So the guardian, whose entire job is independent verification, ends up grading a worker based on that worker's own summary of itself. The proposal is one shared log that every agent writes to. Picture a worker that made forty tool calls. Today that leaves one small file behind. With the new sink switched on, it leaves forty one linked records: the session itself, and every message, thought, call and result inside it, in order. From that log alone the whole run can be reconstructed exactly. And because the new backend is off unless you set two configuration fields, every run that exists today is unchanged, byte for byte."
